#problem()[
  Let $I$ be an ideal in a commutative ring $R$, $I != R$. Show that there is a minimal prime ideal containing $I$. (That means: there is a prime ideal containing $I$ which contains no other prime ideal containing $I$.) Can you translate this statement into geometric terms, about $Spec(R)$? (Remember that there are two different ways to think of prime ideals in terms of $Spec(R)$: they are the points of $Spec(R)$, but they also correspond to the irreducible closed subsets of $Spec(R)$, in a way you can recall.)
]
#solution[
  We will prove this in two steps. We will first show that $(Spec(R), subset.eq)$ has a minimal element, a slightly more general statement. Then we will show why that implies that set of the prime ideals which contain $I subset.eq R$, an ideal, or $V(I)$ has a minimal element.

  1. *$(Spec(R), subset.eq)$* has a minimal element for any commutative ring $R != 0$: Since $R!=0$, it has a maximal ideal meaning $Spec(R) != emptyset$. Let $S$ be a arbitrary chain. Let $ I & = inter.big_(fp in S) fp. $ Since $I$ is the intersection of ideals, we know $I$ is a ideal. Furthermore, we want to show that its prime. Note that $1 in.not frak(p) in S => 1 in.not I$. Now suppose $a, b in.not I$, we will show that $a b in.not I$. Since $a, b in.not I$, there must exists a $fp_a in S$ such that $a in.not fp_a$ and $fp_b in S$ such that $b in.not fp_b$. We have two cases:
    1. Suppose $fp_a subset.eq fp_b$ (using chain property), $b in.not fp_a$. Thus $a, b in.not fp_a => a b in.not fp_a => a b in.not I$.
    2. Suppose $fp_b subset.eq fp_a$, $a in.not fp_b$. Thus $a, b in.not fp_b => a b in.not fp_b => a b in.not I$.
    Thus in either case, we get an implication that $a b in.not I$, meaning that $a b in.not I$. Hence $I$ is prime and $I subset.eq frak(p)$ for all $frak(p) in S$. Hence $I$ is a lower bound for the chain $S$ and $I in Spec(R)$. By Zorn's Lemma, since every chain has a lower bound, $Spec(R)$ has at least 1 minimal element, ie there is a prime ideal $frak(p)$ of $R$ such that it doesn't contain any other prime ideals.
  2. $V(I)$ has a minimal element for $I subset R$ an proper ideal: Note that $ V(I) <-> Spec(lcoset(R, I)) $ where it sends a prime ideal of $lcoset(R, I)$ to its preimage under the quotient map, which is also a prime ideal. Furthermore, the correspondence respects inclusion. Hence a minimal element of $Spec(lcoset(R, I))$ corresponds to a minimal element of $V(I)$, or a prime ideal which contains $I$ but doesn't contain any other prime ideals containing $I$.

  Translating the statement into a topological one, it is equivalent to say that we can partition $Spec(R)$ into maximal irreducible components.
]

#problem()[
  Let $k$ be a field. Show that every ideal in the polynomial ring $k[x]$ is generated by one element. But show that the ideal $(x^n, x^(n-1) y, dots, y^n)$ in $k[x, y]$ cannot be generated by fewer than $n + 1$ elements. Thus there is no upper bound for the number of elements needed to generate an ideal in $k[x, y]$. (Hint: try to reduce this to a question about the dimension of a vector space over a field.)
]
#solution[
  Let $I$ be a ideal in $k[x]$. Note that $0$ is a lower bound for $deg(I)$ and $deg(I) subset.eq NN$ meaning there exists $f in I$ such that $deg(f) <= deg(g)$ for all $g in I / {0}$. I will show that $I = k[x] f$. Since $k[x]$ is a Euclidean domain, $forall g in I$: $g(x) = p(x) f(x) + q(x)$ for $p, q in k[x]$ and $deg(q) < deg(f)$. $q(x) = g(x) - p(x) f(x) in I$ since $deg(f) <= deg(q)$ if $q != 0$, that means $deg(f) > deg(q) => q = 0$. Hence everything in $I$, is a polynomial multiple of $f$. Hence $I = k[x] f = (f)$.

  Let $frak(m) = (x, y)$ and let $I_n = (x^n, x^(n - 1) y, ..., x y^(n - 1) , y^n)$. Let $P_n$ be the $k$-vector space of degree $n$ homogenuous polynomials in two variables, and note that $P_n$ has dimension $n + 1$. Note that $frak(m) I_n subset.eq I_n subset frak(m).$. Note the following $ {Q h: Q in k[x, y] - k, h in P_n} "generate" frak(m) I_n. $ Thus $ f = 0 in (I_n) / (m I_n) <=> f = sum_(k = 1)^(m) Q_k h_k $ where $Q_k in k[x, y] - k$, and $h_k in P_n$. Thus the only nonzero terms are degree $n$ homogenuous polynomials in 2 variables. In fact all nonzero elements $f in P_n$ are nonzero in $lcoset(I_n, frak(m) I_n).$ In less complicated words, $ lcoset(I_n, frak(m) I_n) iso_(k "Vect") P_n. $ Thus if $I_n$ could be generated by less than $n + 1$ elements, so could $lcoset(I_n, frak(m) I_n)$, or equivalently $P_n$, which contradicts the fact that the dimension of $P_n$ is $n + 1$, thus a spanning set must have at least $n + 1$ elements.
]
